# Milk, vinegar, curds and whey: how much acid is enough?
#
# A child-friendly comparison using ordinary milk and 5% household vinegar.
# Do not taste anything from a lab vessel; clean the vessels after the
# activity, and ask an adult before using food for an experiment.
register lv1

# Begin with a deliberately tiny vinegar dose. The milk stays a dispersed
# white colloid because the installed acid dose has not reached its declared
# curdling onset.
add v1 whole_milk 100mL
add v1 white_vinegar_5_percent 1mL
inspect v1

# Make a fair second vessel with the same milk volume and ten times the
# vinegar. The actual acetic-acid inventory now reaches the bounded response:
# milk solids join visible soft clumps while the surrounding whey becomes less
# opaque. This is an educational aggregate model, not a recipe, food-safety
# check, cheese-yield prediction or claim about every kind of milk.
new beaker
add v2 whole_milk 100mL
add v2 white_vinegar_5_percent 10mL
inspect v2
